Written by Artur Nunes.

[#4471](/kb/incidents/4471) said one issue, one fix. The reports in it show three problems, and
each has its own ticket. Engineering closed #4471 on 5 October: the parser is fixed and the
booking-v3 form is retired. That ends two of the three problems. The third is still open.

<ProblemSplit ticket={4471} {issues} {status} />

<Callout kind="warn">
#4471 is closed, but the one-day problem is not. Neither the parser fix nor retiring the form
touches it. Keep its reports on its own ticket.
</Callout>

## The three problems

**`parser`: the shared parser read dates month first.** Every tenant on file writes the day
first. A booking for 7 April went out as 4 July. Fixed: `fix(dates): parse dd/mm for eu locales`.

**`hint`: the booking-v3 form asked for the wrong order.** Its date field said `MM/DD/YYYY`, so
people typed month first, and the form then read the date the tenant's way, day first. The date
came out flipped the same way, so the reports looked like `parser`, but they came only through
the form on booking-v3 tenants. Won't fix: the booking-v3 form is retired, so no new reports can
come this way.

**`one-day`: the date moves by one day.** The invite lands one day early, or one day apart
between clients in different places ("Client in Lisbon got Tuesday. Client in Toronto got
Monday."). A month/day flip cannot do that, so the parser fix does not end it. The desk had noted
these reports (`desk-notes.json`) but filed them under `parser` by tenant. Open.

## How the reports were counted

- By id: the export repeats some reports across pages, so a repeat is counted once.
- By tenant and channel, not by the intake tag. Intake guesses from the reporter's browser
  and is often wrong about which form the report came through.
- A report the desk read as a one-day move counts under `one-day`, and also under its tenant's
  cause, where the desk filed it.

## Lessons

- Matching on subject ([decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject)) merged three
  problems into one ticket. Read the bodies before you call a big ticket known.
- Closing a merged ticket closes only the problems its fix touches. Check each one.

